  "query_preview": "..\nBroad upper level ridging will persist across the inland\nnorthwest through the remainder of the week, with the ridge axis\nmoving onto the Washington coast Thursday, then traversing\neastern Washington Thursday night into Friday. This will allow\ntemperatures to creep upward each day. Highs will increase into\nthe upper 80s to low 90s on Wednesday. Thursday and Friday look\nto be the warmest days of the week, with highs in the mid 90s to\nnear 100 in the mountain valleys. Low temperatures will also\nbegin to rise into the mid 50s to low 60s in the Basin, up to\nthe mid 60s in the valleys. This will increase HeatRisk to\nModerate across most of the area, though some of the valleys\nhave around a 30% to locally 50% of seeing Major HeatRisk. The\nbiggest variable in the temperature forecast will be the smoke\ncoverage. Smoke may other limit high temperatures but maintain \nthe low temperature, which would lower the overall risks due to\nheat.\n\nBy Friday, the upper level ridge will continue to exit to the\neast, allowing the flow to become more zonal and allow weak\nshortwaves to move through the flow. The biggest impact from\nthis pattern change will be the increase in winds. Winds remain\nmostly light before Friday, but then west to southwest winds\nwill begin to pick up during the day on Friday. Winds look to\nease overnight before picking back up again on Saturday. With \ncontinued warm and very dry conditions, this will start to renew\nthe concern for elevated to critical fire weather conditions. \nThe strongest winds are most likely to remain confined to \nportions of central Washington including the east Cascade \nvalleys and the Waterville Plateau. There is around a 40-70% \nchance of wind gusts up to 30-35 mph in those locations in \ncentral WA Friday and Saturday. Elsewhere in the Basin and into \neastern Washington and portions of the northern panhandle, gusts\nin the 15-20 mph is the most likely range. These winds will \nvery likely not be as strong as what was observed on Aug 1, \nespecially east of Grant and Douglas counties. High temperatures\nremain in the low to mid 90s with lows in the upper 50s to mid \n60s. Minimum relative humidity will reach the 10-25% range with \nlimited recovery overnight.\n\nConditions Sunday into early next week look to remain under the\ninfluence of mostly zonal flow with weak shortwaves moving \nthrough. This will allow for temperatures to cool down closer \nto climatological normals, through no chances for \nprecipitation are currently forecast. Winds will remain \ndiurnally driven with the potential for some locally breezy \nwinds through the Cascade gaps. /LH",
